# SSH Drive testbed - environment for `docker compose` (compose.yaml).
#
#     cp .env.example .env      # then edit .env; it is gitignored, this file is not
#
# docker compose reads ./.env automatically when it is run from this directory.
# Only the `ts-ssh` (Tailscale SSH) service needs anything here - but TS_AUTHKEY
# is declared with the required form `${TS_AUTHKEY:?...}`, so with no .env in
# place EVERY compose command in this directory fails, not just that service.

# --- ts-ssh: the Tailscale auth key ------------------------------------------
# Admin console -> Settings -> Keys -> Generate auth key:
#   Reusable      yes       (a re-created container authenticates again)
#   Pre-approved  yes       (only matters if device approval is on for the tailnet)
#   Ephemeral     optional  (the node removes itself when the container stops)
#   Tags          tag:testbed
# The tailnet policy file needs a `tagOwners` entry for tag:testbed and an `ssh`
# rule allowing your devices in as `alec`/`root` - both are in README.md,
# "Tailscale SSH (ts-ssh)". Keys expire (90 days maximum); a rotated key just
# goes here and `docker compose up -d ts-ssh` picks it up.
TS_AUTHKEY=tskey-auth-...

# A tagged auth key applies its own tag, so this is normally left empty.
# Set it to be explicit about the tag the node advertises:
#TS_TAGS=--advertise-tags=tag:testbed

# --- everything else: the address the other eleven services publish on -------
# 192.168.64.1 (the default) is the Mac's vmnet gateway, reachable from the
# build VM.  127.0.0.1 locks the VM out and leaves them Mac-only.
#TESTBED_BIND=192.168.64.1
